1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. Bd3 c5 6. c3 Nc6 7. Ne2 cxd4 8. cxd4 f6 9. exf6 Nxf6 10. O-O Bd6 11. Nf3 Qc7 ({Another common theoretical line is:} 11... O-O 12. Bf4 Bxf4 13. Nxf4 Ne4) 12. Bg5 O-O 13. Bh4 {White wants to play h4-g3 in order to exchange black's good dark-squared bishop. Therefore, black's next move is logical.} ({A solid alternative is:} 13. Rc1) 13... Nh5 (13... e5 14. dxe5 Nxe5 15. Nxe5 Bxe5 16. Bg3 {gives white a slight, but long lasting advantage.}) 14. Qc2 h6 15. Bh7+ Kh8 16. Bg6 Rxf3 {A standard and theoretical exchange sacrifice.} 17. Bxh5 ({White can also accept the sacrifice, for instanse:} 17. gxf3 Bxh2+ 18. Kh1 Nf4 19. Ng3 Bxg3 (19... Qd6 $140 $5 20. Kxh2 $2 Nxd4 21. Qc3 e5 $40) 20. fxg3 Nxg6 21. Qxg6 Nxd4 22. Rac1 Qd7 23. Rf2 $36 {1-0 Volzhin,A (2556)-Johannessen,L (2422)/Bergen 2000/CBM 077 ext (37)}) 17... Bxh2+ $2 {In such a sharp line both sides are obliged to play precisely. The move in the text is the decisive mistake. Black simply won't be able to save both rook and dark-squared bishop. The h2 is of no significance at all in this position.} (17... Rf8 18. Bg3 Bd7 19. Rad1 (19. Rac1 Rac8 20. Qd2 Bxg3 21. hxg3 Qb6 22. Nf4 Kg8 23. Ng6 Rf7 24. Rfd1 Rd8 25. Rc3 $14 {½-½ Sermek,D (2515)-Gleizerov,E (2586)/Saint Vincent 2001/CBM 080 ext (33)}) 19... Bxg3 20. hxg3 Rf6 21. Nf4 Rc8 22. Bg4 Rxf4 23. gxf4 Qxf4 24. Bh3 Nxd4 25. Qd2 Qxd2 26. Rxd2 Nc6 $11 {½-½ Godena,M (2527)-Berescu,A (2439)/Warsaw 2005/CBM 108 (38)}) 18. Kh1 Rf5 (18... Rf8 19. f4 $1 (19. g3 $2 g5 $13) 19... Bxf4 20. Nxf4 Rxf4 21. Bg3 Rxf1+ 22. Rxf1 Qd8 23. Rf7 Nxd4 24. Qf2 $2 (24. Qg6 $1 Nf5 25. Rxg7 $1 Nxg7 26. Qxh6+ Kg8 27. Bg6 {with a mate coming soon.}) 24... Nf5 25. Bg6 Nxg3+ 26. Qxg3 e5 27. Qxe5 Qg8 $14 {½-½ Delgado,N (2557)-Rodriguez,A (2556)/Sao Paulo 2004/CBM 100 ext (53)}) 19. Bg6 Bd6 {Black is forced to sacrifice the exchange to save his dark-squared bishop.} 20. Bxf5 exf5 21. Rae1 $146 (21. f4 $6 Qf7 22. Rf3 Bd7 23. Bf2 Re8 24. Nc3 $14 {½-½ Pan Qian (2196)-Zhukova,N (2479)/Xiapu 2005/CBM 106 ext (56)}) 21... Bd7 (21... g5 $2 22. Bg3 f4 23. Qg6 $1 fxg3 (23... Bf8 24. Nxf4 $1 gxf4 25. Re8 $18) 24. fxg3 Qg7 25. Qxd6 $18) 22. Nc3 Qb6 23. Qd2 f4 {Trying to trap white's dark-squared bishop. The alternatives are not better.} (23... Qxd4 24. Qxd4 Nxd4 25. Be7 $18) (23... g5 24. Bxg5 hxg5 25. Qxg5 {with a decisive attack.}) 24. Nxd5 Qb5 25. Nxf4 Bb4 26. Qd1 Bxe1 27. Rxe1 {Black has managed to win the exchange back, but now there is no real compensation for white's extra pawn. Actually, white's advantage has transformed and even increased.} Qf5 (27... g5 $2 28. Qh5 $18) (27... Qxb2 28. d5 Nd4 29. Re7 (29. Ng6+ Kg8 30. Ne7+ Kh8 31. Qd3 Bb5 32. Qe4 Qd2 {looks also very promising for white, but probably black still holds on somehow...}) 29... Bf5 30. Qe1 ({Does not work} 30. Bf6 $4 gxf6 31. Qh5 Qc1+ 32. Kh2 Qxf4+) 30... Qb1 (30... Nc2 31. Qd2 $18) 31. Qxb1 Bxb1 32. d6 g5 33. d7 $18) 28. Bg3 Re8 29. Rxe8+ Bxe8 30. d5 Ne5 31. f3 Ng6 32. Ne6 {Obviously, white avoids the knight exchange. Black's only drawing chance would be opposite coloured bishops.} Bf7 33. Qd4 $5 {Navara chooses the most solid way. It's a pity for white to exchange his strong passed "d" pawn for black's side pawn. However, in case that Navara was short of time, his decision is the best from practical point of view.} (33. Nc7 Qg5 34. Kh2 h5 35. d6 h4 36. Be1 Nf4 37. Qd2 Qe5 38. Bxh4 $18) 33... Bxe6 34. dxe6 Qxe6 35. Qxa7 Qd5 36. Qa4 Kh7 37. Qe4 Qxa2 38. Qxb7 h5 39. Qb4 $1 {Preventing h5-h4 and restricting black's knight completely.} Qd5 40. Kh2 Qg5 41. Qd4 Ne7 42. Bh4 $1 {Transposing into a completely won endgame.} Nf5 43. Bxg5 Nxd4 44. Kg3 Kg6 45. Be3 Nb3 {Trying to block white's passed pawn, but eventually, because of zugzwang black will have to give way.} 46. Kf4 Kf6 47. Bb6 g6 48. Be3 Ke6 49. Kg5 Kf7 50. f4 Na5 (50... Kg7 51. f5 gxf5 52. Kxf5 $18) 51. f5 gxf5 52. Kxf5 Nc4 53. Bd4 Nd6+ 54. Kg5 h4 55. Bc3 Nc4 56. Kxh4 Ne3 57. g4 Kg6 58. b4 Nd5 59. b5 1-0
